# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE egress-VPN CONFIG-SECRET CONTRACT — the ONE source of truth.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The delivery path has two halves that ship from two
# different repositories and never build together:
#
#   WRITER  hugmesh-admin-bff-api  — Fabric8VpnConfigSecretWriter server-side-
#           applies one K8s Secret per imported VPN config.
#   READER  this repo              — the secret-projector DaemonSet
#           (manifests/egress-vpn/secret-projector-daemonset.yaml) lists those
#           Secrets and writes their bytes onto each assigned node's
#           /etc/hugmesh/vpn-secret, where hugmesh-vpn-reconcile opens
#           ${VPN_SECRET_DIR}/<secretRef>.
#
# Both halves merged green while DISAGREEING on two of the three values below
# (the reader looked in `hugmesh-egress-vpn` for a data key called `config`; the
# writer put the bytes in `hugmesh-backend` under a key named after the Secret).
# Nothing failed, because nothing compared them: each side's tests asserted only
# its own constants. The result was a delivery leg that could never deliver.
#
# So: these three values are declared ONCE, here, and every site that carries a
# copy is asserted against this file by tests/egress-vpn/test-secret-contract.sh
# — INCLUDING the admin-bff source itself, which that test reads out of a sibling
# checkout and refuses to pass without (exit 3 INCOMPLETE, never a silent green).
# Change a value here and every consumer goes red until it follows; change a
# consumer without changing this file and it goes red immediately.
#
# ── PROVENANCE (hugmesh-admin-bff-api @ origin/main, 2026-08-05) ───────────────
#   src/main/java/.../egressvpn/Fabric8VpnConfigSecretWriter.java:69
#       .withName(secretRef)                      -> Secret NAME  == secretRef
#   src/main/java/.../egressvpn/Fabric8VpnConfigSecretWriter.java:72
#       .addToLabels("snapdragon.systems/optional-service", "egress-vpn")
#   src/main/java/.../egressvpn/Fabric8VpnConfigSecretWriter.java:76
#       .addToStringData(secretRef, configBody)   -> DATA KEY     == secretRef
#   src/main/java/.../egressvpn/Fabric8VpnConfigSecretWriter.java:43
#   src/main/java/.../egressvpn/EgressVpnSecretDeliveryConfig.java:36
#   src/main/resources/application.yml:939
#       namespace = ${EGRESS_VPN_SECRET_NAMESPACE:hugmesh-backend}
#                                                 -> NAMESPACE is a DEPLOY LEVER
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ── NAMESPACE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The generator wins this one, and it wins it on the writer's own terms: the
# namespace is the ONLY value of the three that admin-bff exposes as a lever
# (EGRESS_VPN_SECRET_NAMESPACE), and this repo owns the admin-bff Deployment that
# sets it (manifests/admin-bff-api/deployment.yaml). So "whatever admin-bff
# actually writes" IS this value — no admin-bff code change, no drift.
#
# Why not simply adopt the writer's `hugmesh-backend` DEFAULT: the reader is a
# node-privileged DaemonSet that writes root-owned bytes under /etc on the host.
# RBAC has no label selector and `resourceNames` cannot match the DYNAMIC
# per-config names, so the namespace IS the only expressible boundary. Pointing
# it at `hugmesh-backend` would hand that host-writing identity read access to
# every DB / OIDC / federation Secret in the app tier. See
# manifests/egress-vpn/namespace.yaml.
#
# CONSUMERS: manifests/egress-vpn/{namespace,secret-projector-rbac,
# writer-rbac,secret-projector-daemonset}.yaml, apps/13e-egress-vpn.yaml,
# manifests/admin-bff-api/deployment.yaml (EGRESS_VPN_SECRET_NAMESPACE).
EGRESS_VPN_SECRET_NAMESPACE=hugmesh-egress-vpn

# ── LABEL SELECTOR ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Already agreed by both halves; declared here so it cannot quietly stop being
# agreed. Selection is by LABEL and never by name, so the control plane keeps
# minting `egress-vpn-<domain>-<name>` with no generator-side change.
EGRESS_VPN_SECRET_SELECTOR=snapdragon.systems/optional-service=egress-vpn

# ── DATA KEY ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# admin-bff wins this one: `.addToStringData(secretRef, configBody)` is a
# hardcoded, no-lever, merged-and-test-pinned derivation (key == the Secret's own
# metadata.name == secretRef), and it is a defensible design — with key == name a
# PLAIN Secret volume projection already materialises exactly the
# ${VPN_SECRET_DIR}/<secretRef> file the node agent opens, so the projector is
# not the only possible delivery mechanism. The generator's shipped `config`
# could never have matched it.
#
# `@name` is a SENTINEL, not a literal key: it means "the data key equals the
# Secret's own metadata.name". It is safe as a sentinel because it can never
# collide with a real key — the Kubernetes API reference for core/v1 Secret says
# of `data`: "Each key must consist of alphanumeric characters, '-', '_' or
# '.'", and `@` is none of those, so no Secret can ever legitimately carry a key
# spelled `@name`.
#
# To move to a literal key (e.g. `config`) later, the change belongs in
# hugmesh-admin-bff-api Fabric8VpnConfigSecretWriter.java:76 + its
# Fabric8VpnConfigSecretWriterTest expectations FIRST; this file and the reader
# follow. Doing it in the reader alone re-creates the exact break this file
# exists to prevent.
EGRESS_VPN_SECRET_KEY=@name

# ── NODE-ARMING LABEL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# GENERATOR-INTERNAL (unlike the three above, admin-bff has no side of this one)
# — but it is duplicated seven ways and was asserted by nothing, which is the
# same defect as the fourth Secret-contract copy wearing a different hat.
#
# The projector's `nodeSelector` and the label an operator is told to put on a
# node MUST be the same key. If they drift, `kubectl label node <n> <the runbook's
# key>=true` schedules ZERO pods and the failure is invisible: no error, no event,
# no AVC — a DaemonSet with an unsatisfiable nodeSelector is simply quiet, and it
# is indistinguishable from "delivery has not converged yet". This is gate 2 of
# the two dark-by-default gates; a gate you cannot open on purpose is a gate you
# cannot tell from one that is broken.
#
# THIS LINE IS THE DECLARATION, NOT THE AUTHORITY. What a mislabelled node fails
# to match is the DaemonSet's `nodeSelector` mapping, so that is what the guard
# grades every carrier against — including this line. Keep them equal; if they
# ever disagree, the mapping is right and this file is wrong.
#
# DO NOT SPELL IT `snapdragon.systems/vpn-egress`. That is the POD opt-in label
# (NetworkPolicy podSelector, set by hugmesh-vpn-mark), one transposition away
# and a completely different carrier. The guard tells them apart; a hurried
# reader may not.
#
# CONSUMERS: manifests/egress-vpn/secret-projector-daemonset.yaml (the
# nodeSelector mapping AND the "DARK BY DEFAULT" header prose),
# manifests/egress-vpn/kustomization.yaml (runbook step 4 + the resource-line
# note), apps/13e-egress-vpn.yaml (TO ENABLE), optional-services.yaml
# (nodeDeliveryLabel), docs/EGRESS-VPN.md.
EGRESS_VPN_NODE_LABEL=snapdragon.systems/egress-vpn

# ── FILE NAME (derived, not independently settable) ───────────────────────────
# The projected file on the node is named EXACTLY the Secret's metadata.name,
# because hugmesh-vpn-reconcile opens ${VPN_SECRET_DIR}/<secretRef> and the
# writer sets metadata.name = secretRef. Recorded so the equality
# `secretRef == Secret name == data key == projected filename` is written down in
# one place.
EGRESS_VPN_SECRET_NAME_EQUALS=secretRef
